window.docContents[93].resolve({"/Quadratic-core/#Quadratic-Number-Fields-Blueprint--Quadratic-core":{"contents":"\n\nThe standard coordinate model is , implemented as\nQsqrtd d := QuadraticAlgebra ℚ d 0. It is the computational model used for\ntrace, norm, conjugation, discriminants, and splitting calculations.\n\nQuadraticField K is the project-level property that a field over\n is a quadratic extension. It is the abstract object of study;\nthe standard model is a coordinate chart.\n\nQuadraticFieldCat bundles fields satisfying QuadraticField\nand organizes isomorphisms, functors, transport, and classification.\n\nA standard parameter is a squarefree integer  together with an algebra\nequivalence from the abstract field to Qsqrtd d.\n\nEvery abstract quadratic field is\n-algebra-equivalent to Qsqrtd d\nfor some squarefree integer .\n\nThe classification also has a ring-equivalence shadow. This is useful when\ntransporting invariants whose API is stated for ring equivalences rather than\nalgebra equivalences.\n\nA field over  is quadratic if and only if it is isomorphic to one of\nthe normalized standard models.\n\nSquarefree standard parameters are unique: an algebra equivalence between\nQsqrtd d₁ and Qsqrtd d₂ forces  under the normalized hypotheses.\n\nThe coordinate API computes trace and norm directly in\nQsqrtd d. These formulas feed the\nring-of-integers and unit calculations.\n\nThe automorphism group of a quadratic field has the expected dichotomy: the only\nautomorphisms are the identity and conjugation.\n\n","context":"Quadratic Number Fields Blueprint","header":"2. Quadratic core","id":"/Quadratic-core/#Quadratic-Number-Fields-Blueprint--Quadratic-core"}});